WEBVTT

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:34.415
This week the New York City Marathon supplies most of the news and a useful pair of training logs. Andreas Almgren won on his debut after a block built on double threshold days and very long hard runs. Jess McClain finished second on one workout and one structured long run a week.

00:00:34.415 --> 00:00:40.915
So the first part of this episode asks what two very different preparations can and cannot tell anyone.

00:00:41.565 --> 00:01:09.440
After that: the death of a runner in New York and what the research on marathon rhabdomyolysis does and does not say; new ferritin thresholds for iron deficiency; a policy change on injectable peptides; three doping and governance stories; a long look at a young footballer told to give up his sport, and what is known about heart screening; then concurrent training, kidney markers on very high protein diets, Smith machine squats, hot baths, Achilles rehabilitation and one wide-fit shoe.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:27.740
A note on method. Most of the papers below were read at abstract level, and podcasts were read through machine transcripts, so I will say so where it matters. The reading pile had nothing in scope this week, so there is no segment from it. It runs a little under the usual hour, because I have not stretched it to fit.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:46.670
New York, on Sunday the first of November. Andreas Almgren of Sweden won in two hours, six minutes and one second, in his first marathon. Yemaneberhan Crippa of Italy was second in two, six, fourteen.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:02:07.195
Kamohelo Mofolo of Lesotho took third on his twenty first birthday in two, six, twenty six, catching the defending champion Benson Kipruto by just over half a second. Almgren finished four seconds outside the Swedish record, and Marathon Handbook says he is the first man to win New York on debut since Gebre Gebremariam in twenty ten.

00:02:07.845 --> 00:02:18.120
The race was decided very late. Ten men went through thirty five kilometres within a second of each other. Almgren and Crippa were given the same time at twenty five miles.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:37.070
All thirteen seconds of the winning margin came after that point: a twenty sixth mile in four minutes twenty five, against Crippa's four thirty two, and the last three hundred and fifty two metres in fifty six seconds. His second half took one hour, one minute and fifty seven seconds after a first half of one, four, oh four.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:44.545
Marathon Handbook went back through the official splits for every winner since twenty fourteen and found no faster second half.

00:02:45.195 --> 00:02:54.120
The women's race was just as close at the end. Sheila Chepkirui of Kenya won her second title in three years in two, twenty four, thirty six.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:12.645
Jess McClain of the United States was six seconds back in two, twenty four, forty two, and beat the defending champion Hellen Obiri by four tenths of a second, catching her on the short rise in the last two hundred metres. McClain is the first American woman in the top three since Shalane Flanagan in twenty eighteen.

00:03:12.645 --> 00:03:28.420
The first five kilometres took eighteen minutes twenty three, and at thirty five kilometres six women were still together, including Edna Kiplagat, who is forty six. Then Chepkirui ran the next five kilometres in sixteen twenty four, the fastest of the race.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:38.120
Weini Kelati, who had been second at thirty kilometres, lost more than a minute over that stretch and finished seventh in two, twenty six, fifty one on her debut.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:59.445
The race also set a participation record. New York Road Runners gave a preliminary count of sixty one thousand, one hundred and forty six finishers from sixty one thousand, five hundred and twenty nine starters, the first time any marathon has passed sixty thousand. London's mark from April was fifty nine thousand, eight hundred and thirty.

00:03:59.445 --> 00:04:14.995
Of the one thousand nine hundred and twenty additional finishers compared with last year, one thousand seven hundred and eighty six were women. London responds next April with a one-off two-day race and one hundred thousand places, with men and women running on separate days.

00:04:15.645 --> 00:04:32.045
On the same morning in Istanbul, Philimon Kipchumba of Kenya won in two, eight, forty five, taking fifty nine seconds off a course record from twenty nineteen. He caught Shadrack Kenduiywo, who had led by two minutes at twenty five kilometres, just before forty kilometres.

00:04:32.045 --> 00:04:44.070
Seven men broke two ten, in a race where the organisers say only three had ever done so. Kasanesh Baze won the women's race in two, twenty four, forty one, leading an Ethiopian top five.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:05:04.670
At the Javelina Jundred in Arizona, Des Linden ran her first hundred miles. She led the women's race at forty five miles and was still in front at nearly fifty seven, then took eight hours and fifty seven minutes over the last forty miles, close to an hour longer than her first sixty. She finished ninth woman in sixteen hours fifty eight.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:24.820
Heather Jackson defended her title in thirteen hours forty eight, passing Carla Molinaro of Britain after the ninety five mile aid station to win by fifty five seconds. Rob Molke won the men's race in twelve hours eleven, seventy eight seconds off the course record. Conditions were humid, with storms and a peak of seventy five Fahrenheit.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:33.795
I did not get to verified reports of triathlon or strength competition this week, so those are left out rather than given from headlines.

00:05:34.445 --> 00:05:49.245
Ciara Mageean, the Irish middle distance runner and European fifteen hundred metre champion in twenty twenty four, died on Sunday morning. She was thirty four and had been living with stage four bowel cancer since the spring of twenty twenty five.

00:05:49.245 --> 00:06:04.570
She won global medals as a teenager, graduated as a physiotherapist, and in twenty twenty three ran four, fourteen point five eight for the mile in Monaco. Athletics Ireland said she will be remembered for her courage, kindness and incredible spirit.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:28.400
The most useful thing to come out of New York is the record of how Almgren prepared, because he writes his own programme, tests his own lactate and talks about it in detail. Marathon Handbook pulled together his training log, interviews and watch data, the last supplied by COROS, the maker of his watch.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:37.525
That is one athlete, a company's data, and a journalist's reconstruction. It is a description, not evidence that the method caused the result.

00:06:38.175 --> 00:06:57.350
Over the ten weeks after his European ten thousand metre title he ran two thousand and six kilometres, with a highest week of two hundred and forty one. He kept two double threshold days a week but changed both sessions. The morning short repetitions became fifty minutes of continuous running at his first lactate threshold.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:14.200
The afternoon session became longer, for example sixteen times one kilometre. On the first of September he ran the first eight of those at two fifty per kilometre with a reading of one point five millimoles, and the last eight at two forty seven with a reading of about two and a half.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:25.875
In one documented week he also ran three fifty minute sessions in a heat chamber at thirty eight degrees and sixty percent humidity, holding his core temperature at about thirty eight and a half.

00:07:26.525 --> 00:07:35.275
The change the article considers most significant was the long run. Before this block he had never run farther than about thirty two kilometres.

00:07:35.275 --> 00:07:52.025
He then did a series of hard long runs, including a forty kilometre run divided into four tens of thirty five minutes, thirty one twenty, twenty nine forty six and twenty eight thirty, and he held back slightly during them so that he could recover within two days and do more of them.

00:07:52.675 --> 00:08:04.475
The race data matches the training claim. The paces he ran in New York correspond, in Marathon Handbook's reading, to paces where his training lactate was between about one and one and a half millimoles.

00:08:04.475 --> 00:08:20.450
His average heart rate was one hundred and fifty six in the first half and one hundred and sixty three in the second, against a threshold heart rate COROS had measured at one hundred and sixty eight in August, while his pace went from three oh two to two fifty six per kilometre.

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:31.450
Cadence rose from one hundred and seventy eight to one hundred and eighty three, and ground contact time fell from one hundred and forty seven milliseconds to one hundred and forty one.

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:44.600
Over the final six hundred metres the watch shows two thirty seven per kilometre and one hundred and twenty milliseconds of contact, though a watch measuring six hundred metres of its own distance has some margin of error.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:55.575
The official splits support the broad point: from forty kilometres to the line he averaged about two forty four and a half, a little quicker than his fifty eight minute half marathon pace.

00:08:55.575 --> 00:09:12.250
My reading, not the article's: heart rate below threshold for most of a marathon at this speed says something about how far he was from his limit, but single-race heart rate is affected by heat, drift and excitement, so I would not treat seven beats as a measurement of anything very precise.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:31.600
His fuelling plan is worth reporting with care, since these are plans he described and not confirmed intake: a bicarbonate drink two hours before the start, a caffeine gel before the start and another at halfway, and a bottle at each five kilometre station through thirty five, each delivering about thirty grams.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:42.350
He had practised at about one hundred and twenty grams of carbohydrate an hour, even on easy long runs. Episode nine covered a bicarbonate trial over ten kilometres.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:56.925
Here is another, from Feuerbacher and colleagues with Bent Rønnestad, in the European Journal of Sport Science: a randomised, double blind crossover in twenty four well trained cyclists taking about zero point two six grams per kilogram.

00:09:56.925 --> 00:10:09.875
Power output in high intensity intervals was two percent higher, and time above ninety percent of V O two max rose by about twenty four seconds. Cycling economy and fractional utilisation did not change.

00:10:09.875 --> 00:10:19.675
The authors say plainly that whether this turns into better long term adaptation has not been shown, and nobody has tested it in a marathon debut in New York.

00:10:20.325 --> 00:10:37.100
Now the other preparation. Jess McClain is coached by David Roche, and on the Some Work, All Play podcast the Roches described her week: one quality workout and one structured long run that serves as a second workout, with truly easy days, and by their account no huge mileage.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:46.575
In the machine transcript the hosts say her easy days have no hidden work in them, and that when this was described to reporters after the race the reaction was surprise.

00:10:46.575 --> 00:11:02.300
Marathon Handbook adds that she still works a job outside running, and gives her record: second in New York, after two twenty seven nineteen in the same race two years ago, and two twenty forty nine at Boston in April, on a course that is not record eligible.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:18.025
The strongest case for Almgren's method is that threshold work controlled by lactate allows a very large volume of quality with little damage, and that a thirty one year old whose injuries kept him out of three Olympic Games is now running better than ever.

00:11:18.025 --> 00:11:35.450
The strongest case for McClain's is that she has kept improving at every major on a much simpler week, after a sacral stress fracture in twenty twenty two changed how she approached training. I cannot compare them. They differ in sex, age, event history, life constraints, and in almost every other variable.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:48.775
The Roches themselves, on the podcast, say Almgren's work at his first threshold resembles what they try to do with uphill treadmill sessions: a lot of output with less breakdown. That is a coach's analogy, not a result.

00:11:48.775 --> 00:12:06.025
Steve Magness's podcast this week, The Good-Enough Approach to Double Threshold, argues, going by the show's own description, that the value of the Norwegian method lies in the principle rather than the copied sessions, and it names survivorship bias. I have read the description, not a transcript.

00:12:06.675 --> 00:12:25.225
The sharpest disagreement this week comes from triathlon. Joel Filliol, who has coached four athletes to seven world titles, was on That Triathlon Show. His squad uses a flat training structure, does few lab tests and no lactate testing, and spreads its hard sessions across the week. His central claim is about dose.

00:12:25.875 --> 00:12:29.300
Here is Joel Filliol on That Triathlon Show.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:52.900
[quoted clip]

00:12:54.242 --> 00:13:10.467
Asked about the threshold volume described by Marius Bakken on the same show two weeks earlier, Filliol said on the podcast that the amount of it, in his words, feels like a lot to me, in the context he works in, because triathletes are recovering from far more total energy use.

00:13:11.117 --> 00:13:24.142
Filliol is talking about triathletes, with far more total training hours, so this is not a straight contradiction of Bakken, whose method is built around running. He said so himself, and he said he finds the book interesting.

00:13:24.142 --> 00:13:36.842
But the two do differ on how much intensity is needed and how it should be controlled: Bakken by lactate and muscle feel, Filliol by pace ranges from Jack Daniels' tables, with a fixed ceiling on the final block.

00:13:36.842 --> 00:13:52.092
He also said he trusts readiness scores less than most athletes do, calling them black box algorithms that can cause more stress than they solve. All of this is a coach's experience. Filliol has the titles, and Almgren is this week's example for the other side.

00:13:52.092 --> 00:14:09.492
Marco Altini made a compatible point about individual differences, in his report from a fifty two kilometre mountain race: he improved by almost an hour with no detectable change in the usual determinants of road performance, which he attributes to specific volume and mechanical durability.

00:14:09.492 --> 00:14:16.542
That is one person's account, from a careful observer, and he says plainly it does not necessarily apply to others.

00:14:17.192 --> 00:14:36.842
One more paper fits here, because it is about concentrated intensity. Sørensen and colleagues, again from the Rønnestad group, assigned seventeen well trained female cross country skiers and biathletes either to five high intensity sessions over six days followed by five days of active recovery, or to their regular training.

00:14:36.842 --> 00:14:49.542
The block group improved more on two of three roller ski performance measures, by three point six percent against one point two and by four point four percent against zero, and the third did not differ significantly.

00:14:49.542 --> 00:15:06.267
On the Fast Talk podcast this week the panel criticised what appears to be a different Rønnestad block study in cyclists, which I could not locate, on the grounds that one group came into the post test rested and the other did not. I raise it because the same question applies here.

00:15:06.267 --> 00:15:21.842
The abstract says the block replaced part of habitual training and reduced overall training load, and the recovery period is part of the protocol. My inference: some of that gain may be freshness on test day. A longer wash-out would be needed to separate the two.

00:15:27.682 --> 00:15:48.632
The saddest story of the week first. Rachel Apa, twenty four, collapsed near mile twenty four of the New York City Marathon, her first. Spectators began C P R and race medical staff used a defibrillator. She died that evening at Mount Sinai after about six hours of treatment. Her father told the New York Times the cause was rhabdomyolysis.

00:15:48.632 --> 00:15:58.807
As Marathon Handbook notes, everything known so far about the cause comes from her family. There is no published medical finding, and I will not speculate about it.

00:15:59.457 --> 00:16:15.432
There is one directly relevant paper, published in April in Nephron by physicians from Mount Sinai and Lenox Hill. It reviewed records at four New York hospitals from twenty seventeen to twenty twenty three for marathon runners with a creatine kinase above five thousand.

00:16:15.432 --> 00:16:33.107
In twenty twenty two, the hottest New York marathon on record, there were twenty four cases, against fifteen in the other five races combined. Cases correlated strongly with race day heat index, and the authors suggest the jump in temperature compared with the previous fortnight may add risk.

00:16:33.107 --> 00:16:48.157
The twenty twenty two cases were mostly men, twenty of twenty four, averaged thirty two years old and came in within two days. One needed dialysis. This is a retrospective chart review that can only find people who came to those hospitals.

00:16:48.157 --> 00:16:57.282
This year's race was much cooler, so the heat finding does not obviously apply, and one case cannot be explained by a population study either way.

00:16:57.932 --> 00:17:11.407
Second, iron. The American Society of Hematology has issued its first evidence-based guideline on diagnosing iron deficiency. It is in the November issue of Blood Advances, after appearing online earlier.

00:17:11.407 --> 00:17:29.207
It recommends a ferritin threshold of thirty nanograms per millilitre or lower for most adults, double the fifteen commonly used, with up to fifty appropriate for adults with symptoms or ongoing risk factors, whether or not they are anaemic. That is a large change, and the caveats are large too.

00:17:29.207 --> 00:17:50.007
According to a clinical summary I read, all five recommendations were conditional, resting on low or very low certainty evidence, except a strong recommendation against using fifteen in pregnancy, which was also low certainty. The authors admit limited direct evidence tying specific ferritin values to outcomes that matter to patients.

00:17:50.007 --> 00:18:07.907
It is not written for athletes. My inference for endurance athletes: more results will now read as deficient, which is useful only if someone also asks why and whether treatment changes anything. Ferritin is an acute phase reactant, so a reading taken soon after a hard race may overstate stores.

00:18:07.907 --> 00:18:19.032
On the podcast covered later in this episode, Austin Baraki said the revision matches what he had already been arguing for in practice. That is a physician's opinion, not trial evidence.

00:18:19.682 --> 00:18:29.332
Third, a development on a story from episode nine. Then it was a JAMA viewpoint on the F D A's advisory committee and injectable peptides.

00:18:29.332 --> 00:18:45.232
On the twenty eighth of October the US Department of Health and Human Services issued an enforcement discretion policy for compounding six of them: B P C one five seven, epitalon, K P V, M O T S C, semax and T B five hundred.

00:18:45.232 --> 00:18:57.582
According to Fierce Pharma, compounders must meet sourcing conditions and report serious adverse events, and the department may revisit the policy if safety signals appear. These are not approved drugs.

00:18:57.582 --> 00:19:13.282
They have not been formally added to the bulk substances list, so this is a decision not to enforce, rather than a finding that they work or are safe. Supporters call it a more accountable route than the grey market, and critics say it undercuts the F D A's own resistance.

00:19:13.282 --> 00:19:28.907
My inference: an American health agency's enforcement stance has no bearing on anti-doping rules, and I did not check the status of each compound on the prohibited list this run, so nobody should assume from this news that any of them is permitted in tested sport.

00:19:29.557 --> 00:19:43.807
Fourth, retatrutide. Eli Lilly released topline results from a head to head phase three trial against injectable semaglutide, in adults with type two diabetes, starting at an average of about one hundred and two kilograms.

00:19:43.807 --> 00:20:03.107
At eighty weeks A one C fell by two point two points against one point eight, and body weight by nineteen and a half percent on nine milligrams and twenty point nine percent on twelve, against nine point four percent. Discontinuation for adverse events was four point eight and seven point nine percent, against two point seven.

00:20:03.107 --> 00:20:17.657
This is a company press release, these are efficacy estimand figures, and the details have not been peer reviewed. The part of the release I read says nothing about how much of that weight was lean mass, which is what would matter most to anyone who trains.

00:20:17.657 --> 00:20:32.957
Fast Talk's panel this week took on whether endurance athletes should use these drugs at all, and said it could find no specific research in endurance athletes. They were clear that the drugs help people at metabolic risk, and that most people regain the weight on stopping.

00:20:32.957 --> 00:20:38.382
I agree that the evidence for using them to get lighter for racing does not exist.

00:20:39.032 --> 00:20:58.357
Fifth, something about how anyone finds evidence, including this programme. Retraction Watch reports that the National Library of Medicine has paused indexing Scientific Reports in MEDLINE, and archiving in PubMed Central, while it re-evaluates the journal. Its editor's email cites significant growth in recent years.

00:20:58.357 --> 00:21:18.232
The most recent articles in PubMed are from the fifteenth of September. The journal has twenty eight thousand, five hundred and ninety three articles indexed in PubMed this year, and has published forty one thousand, one hundred and twenty two. The library's policy says re-evaluation typically takes seven to ten months, and it did not give a reason.

00:21:18.232 --> 00:21:27.807
My inference: for this digest, whose literature searches run largely on PubMed, any Scientific Reports paper since mid September will simply not appear.

00:21:33.997 --> 00:21:54.097
A follow-up to episode nine, when Peres Jepchirchir's provisional suspension and Ruti Aga's six year ban were the news. The Athletics Integrity Unit has now banned Mosisa Siyoum of Ethiopia for eight years. Four are for E P O in a sample taken after he won a mile in Saudi Arabia last December, and four are for tampering.

00:21:54.097 --> 00:22:11.247
He had explained the positive with medical records saying a health centre gave him erythropoietin for anaemia. According to Marathon Handbook's account of the decision, the centre replied that the record number was not in its format, the signatory was not on its staff, and he had not been there on that date.

00:22:11.247 --> 00:22:24.122
His three thirty four point two eight personal best is gone. He did not contest it, though an appeal to the Court of Arbitration for Sport remains possible. Fake paperwork has now added four years twice in a fortnight.

00:22:24.772 --> 00:22:39.697
Jepchirchir's case is still provisional, and the sample came from an out-of-competition test in Kenya about six weeks before her Sydney win. Athletics Kenya has restated a zero tolerance policy. Two countries have moved on the suppliers.

00:22:39.697 --> 00:22:56.022
Kenya's Anti-Doping Amendment Bill, now at public hearings, would raise minimum penalties for supplying or administering banned substances to five million shillings, about thirty eight thousand dollars, or five years in prison, or both, up from three million and three years.

00:22:56.022 --> 00:23:16.272
It covers doctors, pharmacists and physiotherapists, and a separate clause sets minimum penalties for people using banned substances in gyms. India's sports minister says a bill creating criminal penalties for suppliers goes to the next session of Parliament, with up to five years in prison, and athletes themselves would not be prosecuted.

00:23:16.272 --> 00:23:23.797
By April, India had one hundred and forty eight athletes on the Athletics Integrity Unit's list, two more than Kenya.

00:23:24.447 --> 00:23:41.297
The Real Science of Sport spent half an hour on what this all means, and the disagreement there is useful. The strongest version of the case for hard sanctions is that Kenya produces a steady run of positives at the top of the sport and has just been awarded the twenty twenty nine world championships.

00:23:41.297 --> 00:23:57.922
Ross Tucker, on the podcast, rejects stripping them. His argument is about incentives: if hosting depends on fewer positive tests, the cheapest way to get them is to catch fewer people, so punishment falls on the body that has let testers work rather than on those supplying drugs.

00:23:57.922 --> 00:24:08.047
The hosts also recall Brett Clothier of the Athletics Integrity Unit describing Kenyan doping as diffuse rather than centrally organised, a market problem.

00:24:08.047 --> 00:24:27.472
That is not the same emphasis as WADA president Witold Bańka's, as quoted on the show, that this should not be seen as only a Kenyan or African problem, although both end up pointing at suppliers. Tucker separately argues against banning agents merely by association, while saying they should be pursued if there is evidence.

00:24:27.472 --> 00:24:43.172
And he reads, from a message sent to him, that the twenty twenty seven World Anti-Doping Code will require automatic investigation when support personnel have worked with more than one athlete who commits a violation. I have not checked that against the Code itself.

00:24:43.172 --> 00:24:49.997
These are opinions from a machine transcript without speaker labels, so treat the attribution as approximate.

00:24:50.647 --> 00:25:11.097
World Athletics, meanwhile, has created a department for road running and mass participation, led by Jakob Larsen. More than three hundred and twenty road races hold a label. Under the twenty twenty seven rules a Platinum marathon pays seventy five thousand dollars, up from fifty thousand, with a two percent levy on top prize money.

00:25:11.097 --> 00:25:26.947
World Athletics says this brings in three million dollars a year for road racing's anti-doping programme, and a Platinum race must also fund at least twelve in-competition tests. My inference: whether three million dollars a year is sized to the problem is a fair question.

00:25:32.777 --> 00:25:49.277
The best hour of audio this week is the Barbell Medicine podcast's mystery case, with Jordan Feigenbaum presenting a published case to Austin Baraki blind. It is a case report from Portugal, so it is one person. A young soccer player with no symptoms needs a form signed.

00:25:49.277 --> 00:26:10.877
His E C G shows voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy, with inverted T waves across many leads and S T depression. Imaging shows thickening at the apex of the left ventricle. Everything functional looks fine: no arrhythmia on the monitor, an exceptional treadmill test, no outflow obstruction, and a negative panel of ten genes.

00:26:10.877 --> 00:26:20.452
He is told to stop. After four months without sport, the E C G has improved and the wall has thinned enough that it no longer meets the diagnostic threshold.

00:26:21.102 --> 00:26:40.302
Most people would now call that athlete's heart, since the textbook test is to remove the training and see what regresses. The title of the case report says otherwise: beware of regression, it may not always mean athlete's heart. The report's authors say it challenges the dogma that such regression is exclusive to physiological remodelling.

00:26:40.302 --> 00:26:56.652
His team kept the diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and he stopped playing. The podcast then gives a second case, of a young hockey player whose thickening regressed substantially, but who had a gene mutation and scarring on imaging, and later needed a defibrillator.

00:26:56.652 --> 00:27:06.127
Feigenbaum's conclusion is that detraining can help rule the disease in but cannot rule it out. That is a clinician's inference from a handful of cases.

00:27:06.777 --> 00:27:20.952
Reference ranges are the next problem. The classic upper limit comes from Pelliccia and colleagues in nineteen ninety one, which episode six discussed, in Italian athletes. Feigenbaum points out this is not the whole athletic population.

00:27:20.952 --> 00:27:33.852
Basavarajaiah and colleagues, in a British study, compared three hundred nationally ranked Black male athletes with three hundred matched white male athletes. Mean wall thickness was eleven point three against ten millimetres.

00:27:33.852 --> 00:27:44.252
Eighteen percent of the Black athletes had walls over twelve millimetres against four percent of the white athletes, and three percent had walls of fifteen or more against none.

00:27:44.252 --> 00:28:01.852
The authors warn that applying limits from white athletes could generate false positive diagnoses, and they also note that deaths from hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are more prevalent among Black athletes, so the error can run in both directions. All male, and that is a real limit of the study.

00:28:02.502 --> 00:28:06.727
Riding and colleagues then showed the word Black covers too much.

00:28:06.727 --> 00:28:24.702
In one thousand six hundred and ninety eight male athletes, left ventricular hypertrophy appeared in nine and a half percent of African American and Caribbean athletes and five percent of West Africans, but none of the East or North Africans, a result that held after accounting for body size.

00:28:24.702 --> 00:28:35.802
Abnormal repolarisation ranged from about one to eight and a half percent by region. Their conclusion is that the collective term should not imply that all Black athletes' hearts are comparable.

00:28:35.802 --> 00:28:49.327
My inference: anyone reading an athlete's E C G or echo needs ancestry more specific than a census box, and someone who has been given an alarming result is entitled to ask which reference population was used.

00:28:49.977 --> 00:29:08.352
Then the question of whether a diagnosis should end a career. Per the show, for forty years guidelines largely said yes. Lampert and colleagues followed one thousand six hundred and sixty people with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or the genotype, at forty two centres. Forty two percent exercised vigorously.

00:29:08.352 --> 00:29:25.202
The composite of death, cardiac arrest, arrhythmic syncope and appropriate shocks occurred in four point seven percent of vigorous exercisers and four point six percent of the rest, an adjusted hazard ratio of one point oh one, with an upper confidence limit of one point four eight.

00:29:25.202 --> 00:29:40.402
Exercise was self-reported and not randomised, people chose their own activity, and they were treated at experienced centres, as the authors say. So this cannot show that vigorous exercise is safe for someone assessed elsewhere, or for every phenotype.

00:29:40.402 --> 00:30:00.202
Per the show, the twenty twenty five American Heart Association and American College of Cardiology statement no longer treats the diagnosis as an absolute bar. Baraki and Feigenbaum both think this patient's low risk features might support shared decision making today, though Baraki says it is outside his routine practice.

00:30:00.852 --> 00:30:21.127
Should an E C G be mandatory? The two pieces of evidence pull apart. Harmon and colleagues pooled fifteen studies and forty seven thousand athletes: E C G sensitivity ninety four percent against twenty percent for history and nine percent for examination, with a lower false positive rate. So it is by far the better detector.

00:30:21.127 --> 00:30:40.102
Halkin and colleagues modelled copying the Italian programme in the United States: between ten point six and fourteen point four million dollars per life saved. The modelling rests on one Italian study, and it is an American cost calculation. Being good at detecting disease is not the same as saving lives economically.

00:30:40.102 --> 00:30:47.477
Feigenbaum is not opposed to the test, only to making amateurs pay for it, and he then says what he would do instead.

00:30:48.127 --> 00:30:52.452
Here is Jordan Feigenbaum on the Barbell Medicine podcast.

00:30:54.132 --> 00:31:18.132
[quoted clip]

00:31:19.474 --> 00:31:40.399
He adds a reason: the studies cited on the show suggest bystanders do not always start C P R, and a defibrillator is used in only a minority of cases. I have not opened those studies. In the story earlier in this episode, spectators did start C P R and race staff did reach her with a defibrillator. Those do not guarantee survival.

00:31:40.399 --> 00:31:44.774
They are, though, measures that a club or a race can actually organise.

00:31:50.954 --> 00:32:10.679
Gomes and colleagues in the Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research randomised thirty trained young men to endurance, resistance or concurrent training for eleven weeks, with a shift in the second part from maximal to explosive strength. Maximal isometric leg press force rose only in the resistance group, by eighteen percent.

00:32:10.679 --> 00:32:30.979
Peak rate of force development improved in both resistance and concurrent groups, but early phase force development in the concurrent group improved only in the second block. Cross-sectional area of the vastus lateralis rose seventeen percent with resistance training and eight point eight with concurrent, both significant, with wide variation.

00:32:30.979 --> 00:32:47.529
Jump impulse improved in both. So there is interference in maximal force and perhaps size, and the authors see none in jump performance. Ten or so men per arm can only give a rough idea, and the abstract does not give a direct comparison between arms for every outcome.

00:32:48.179 --> 00:33:01.304
Trane and colleagues, from Norway, randomised fifty eight recreationally active people not trained in strength to two weekly sprint sessions, alone or with heavy squats, hypertrophy squats, or squat jumps, for eight weeks.

00:33:01.304 --> 00:33:16.579
Running economy improved two and a half percent with heavy squats, more than with sprinting alone, and leg press force by fourteen and a half percent. Short sprint time improved by about a tenth of a second with either kind of squat. Squat jumps did not match them.

00:33:16.579 --> 00:33:35.429
Note the authors' own words: an exploratory design with no strong multiplicity control, and hypothesis generating. These were not runners, and sprint work is not distance training. It does fit a long-standing idea that heavy lifting can help economy, but this paper adds a suggestion rather than a confirmation.

00:33:41.619 --> 00:33:59.819
Very high protein and kidney markers first. Bagheri and colleagues in the Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition randomised forty eight resistance trained men to resistance or concurrent training, for sixteen weeks, on either one point six or three point two grams of protein per kilogram.

00:33:59.819 --> 00:34:14.569
There were no significant time by protein interactions for any renal marker or eGFR estimate, whichever equation was used. Within the three point two groups, urea nitrogen and cystatin C rose, and eGFR estimates fell.

00:34:14.569 --> 00:34:30.644
Those are the within-group results a press release would headline, and the authors correctly say they are descriptive because no interaction supports them. Their other caveat matters just as much: with forty four men analysed, the study cannot exclude clinically meaningful differences.

00:34:30.644 --> 00:34:49.694
Reasonable summary: no signal of harm over four months in healthy young men, which is a long way from proof of safety. One useful detail is that the authors measured cystatin C, which does not depend on muscle mass the way creatinine does, so this rise is harder to dismiss than a creatinine rise in a large lifter.

00:34:49.694 --> 00:34:55.344
That last point is my own, and it remains a small change without a clinical endpoint.

00:34:55.994 --> 00:35:06.844
Next, squats. On Iron Culture this week Eric Trexler and Eric Helms took a question prompted by a viral video of a professional bodybuilder's spinal injury in a Smith machine.

00:35:06.844 --> 00:35:26.144
The hazard, as they describe it, is a specific one: with a barbell a failed rep can be dumped, while on a Smith machine the load only moves down, so the safety stoppers need setting correctly. They argue it should be seen as a separate machine rather than a poor copy of a barbell squat, and a very quad dominant movement if set up that way.

00:35:26.144 --> 00:35:30.244
These are coaches' views, and they do not give an injury rate.

00:35:30.894 --> 00:35:43.794
A paper from Granada adds something. Marcos-Frutos and colleagues had nineteen strength trained men do four sets of nine at seventy percent of one rep maximum, with free weight and Smith squats in random order.

00:35:43.794 --> 00:35:58.069
Velocity loss and perceived fatigue did not differ, but twitch interpolation showed more peripheral and central fatigue in the knee extensors after free weights. The authors suggest the Smith squat could be more efficient. Two cautions, in my reading.

00:35:58.069 --> 00:36:17.844
A single session measuring only the knee extensors says nothing about adaptation, and their premise that adaptations are comparable comes from other work. Also, seventy percent on each implement may be a different relative demand depending on how the one rep maximum was tested, and I cannot see from the abstract how that was handled.

00:36:18.494 --> 00:36:36.694
Alyssa Olenick's Hybrid Lab this week is a short and sensible antidote to worry about lifting technique, arguing that form is a skill to develop under loads the lifter can handle, and that most problems come from doing too much too soon. That is coaching opinion. One perspective paper is worth a sentence too.

00:36:36.694 --> 00:36:53.869
Augustsson, in the journal Sports, cites evidence that only two point three percent of participants in a set of highly cited strength reviews were highly trained or elite. That is a fair warning about reading anything here, including the studies above, as advice for experienced lifters.

00:36:59.709 --> 00:37:19.884
A short item, and thin evidence. Callegari and colleagues, also in the Journal of the International Society of Sports Nutrition, meta-analysed plant-based versus whey supplementation with resistance training in untrained adults, measured by D E X A. Both groups gained lean mass and lost fat with no significant difference.

00:37:19.884 --> 00:37:35.634
The soy and pea evidence supports that. Chia rests on a single study that showed no significant change. Five studies in untrained people is not much, and beginners gain muscle under almost any adequate protein arrangement, which I should say is my gloss.

00:37:35.634 --> 00:37:51.159
It sits alongside the network meta-analysis from episode nine, for anyone on a plant-based diet: pea and soy look like reasonable substitutes for whey in this setting, the abstract gives no pooled effect size, and trained lifters remain largely unstudied.

00:37:57.339 --> 00:38:14.339
Keefe and colleagues in the Journal of Applied Physiology studied eighteen highly trained endurance runners, eight men and ten women. They did seven sessions of an hour at fifty percent of velocity at V O two max in forty degrees, and seven in twenty two degrees, in random order.

00:38:14.339 --> 00:38:34.289
There were no sex differences in the trajectory or size of adaptation in core temperature, heart rate or sweat rate. Heat produced larger falls in core temperature and heart rate and larger rises in sweat rate than temperate running, though temperate running alone produced some adaptation. Plasma volume did not change under either condition.

00:38:34.289 --> 00:38:50.339
Eight men and ten women is not enough to rule out a modest sex difference, and that is my caution rather than the authors'. Still, it is a useful crossover design in the population that matters, at a controlled work rate, with an interesting absence of plasma volume change.

00:38:50.989 --> 00:39:11.289
Next, baths. Zerilli and colleagues at Cardiff Metropolitan, in Experimental Physiology, put sixteen healthy adults, four of them women, through forty five minutes at forty degrees and a time-matched control. Plasma volume fell nine and a half percent immediately after, then returned to baseline within twelve hours with no later expansion.

00:39:11.289 --> 00:39:26.789
Erythropoietin did not change over twenty four hours. The first author, Owain Zerilli, took the paper through on The Physiology of Endurance Running this week with his co-host and co-author Dan Nash, and it is a good account of how a master's project becomes a paper.

00:39:26.789 --> 00:39:41.114
They contrast it with a paper from DeMarco and colleagues in Oregon that found an increase at six hours, in women, but without a control condition. Zerilli says the aim of an equal sample was not met, so this one cannot settle the sex question.

00:39:41.114 --> 00:39:59.064
The abstract's interpretation is that adaptation from repeated bathing may come from sustained changes in plasma volume rather than a pulse of erythropoietin after each bath. A single bout is a narrow test of that, and my own thought is that a serum assay at set time points could miss a brief pulse.

00:39:59.714 --> 00:40:20.564
And an open item closed. In episode nine I could report a photobiomodulation study only through Alex Hutchinson's column. I have now found it. Aussieker and colleagues from Luc van Loon's group, in Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise, treated one leg of twelve people with light and the other with sham, and took biopsies of skin and muscle.

00:40:20.564 --> 00:40:38.914
Skin warmed by eight degrees. Mitochondrial respiration was not different in either tissue, and of ninety one genes only three differed. That is one session, and tissue measured outside the body. Hutchinson's description holds. The hot tub versus ice bath paper from the same column is still not on PubMed.

00:40:45.094 --> 00:40:56.494
Doctors of Running this week did something reviewers rarely do and talked about their own injuries. Matt Klein has had Achilles tendinopathy for over a year, and here is what he says he learned.

00:40:57.144 --> 00:41:00.869
Here is Matt Klein on the Doctors of Running podcast.

00:41:02.549 --> 00:41:28.089
[quoted clip]

00:41:29.431 --> 00:41:45.756
Klein is a physical therapist describing his own case, and he also says he should have taken time off early and did not. He later describes regaining ankle motion after about a year, with a flare along the way, and loading into that range as what helped most. That is one tendon.

00:41:45.756 --> 00:41:56.881
His distinction between the two common types of the injury, mid-portion and insertional, is standard teaching. It is also a reasonable warning against generic advice to stretch.

00:41:57.531 --> 00:42:17.456
A small lab study from Japan is relevant to the shoe side. Sato and colleagues in The Foot had nineteen healthy men who land on their heels run in neutral, rocker and advanced footwear technology shoes. Rocker shoes reduced peak Achilles force and force impulse per step. The advanced shoes did not differ from neutral on any Achilles measure.

00:42:17.456 --> 00:42:30.831
Those are healthy men at one controlled speed, and the abstract gives no numbers. Nothing here shows that rockers help anyone already injured, but it does tell against the casual assumption that a super shoe relieves the Achilles.

00:42:37.021 --> 00:42:49.146
The digest's standing focus on wide fittings narrows this week's list to one new review. The H O K A Arahi nine, reviewed by Doctors of Running, is a stability shoe for walking and easy running.

00:42:49.146 --> 00:43:09.146
It has a forty one millimetre heel, thirty three millimetre forefoot, an eight millimetre drop, and a men's weight of nine point seven ounces, at one hundred and fifty dollars in the United States. The review describes no plate, but an H frame within the compression moulded foam sets the ride. The upper is a full length mesh with mild stretch.

00:43:09.146 --> 00:43:26.471
Reviewers describe the toe box as a bit tapered, particularly on the medial side, the midfoot as normal to slightly snug, and the forefoot as not overly wide, though with more room than the last version. Those impressions come from two testers, and the summary lists a firm midsole as a drawback.

00:43:26.471 --> 00:43:43.271
Matt Klein's verdict is that it fits normal to narrow feet best and that wider feet should consider the wide version. In the United Kingdom, the retailer Moti Running lists a men's wide at one hundred and forty pounds, and Northern Runner lists a women's wide at one hundred and forty pounds in-store.

00:43:43.271 --> 00:43:52.621
Moti's listing says wide without giving a letter fitting. The H O K A U K site would not load for me, so I have not confirmed the manufacturer's list.

00:43:53.271 --> 00:44:06.271
Two other things on width. SportsShoes dot com lists the New Balance ten eighty version fifteen in both two E and four E widths, and, unusually for a racing shoe, the SuperComp Elite version six in two E.

00:44:06.271 --> 00:44:21.771
Doctors of Running reviewed that racer in July: a forty millimetre heel, thirty two millimetre forefoot, eight millimetre drop, one hundred and ninety grams in a men's nine and a half, and a price of two hundred and seventy five dollars in the United States.

00:44:21.771 --> 00:44:38.396
It has a plate that is thicker and stiffer in the forefoot, an upper of thin mesh with plastic overlays, and a toe box they call not particularly roomy, tapered laterally, but more comfortable than many racers. They warn that the narrow rearfoot and midfoot platform may be unstable for some.

00:44:38.396 --> 00:44:48.871
The portion of the review I read does not cover the wide version, so I cannot say how the fit changes in it. A retailer's listing is evidence of availability and nothing more.

00:44:49.521 --> 00:45:01.671
The rest gets a sentence, with no wide fitting confirmed: Almgren won in the Nike Apex, which goes on sale in January, and Northern Runner reviewed the Mount to Coast M one and the Altra Vanish Pulse.

00:45:02.321 --> 00:45:20.346
On devices, one white paper is worth reading sceptically, because it is the one place this week that skin tone comes up in wearable accuracy. Temple, a company selling a sensor worn at the temple, published an internal study of forty three people against a Polar H ten chest strap on a treadmill.

00:45:20.346 --> 00:45:40.621
Temple reports its own mean absolute error at about one point two beats a minute, with Apple Watch Series twelve at one point nine, Fitbit Air at three, WHOOP and Garmin Forerunner nine seventy at a little over three, and Oura Ring five at five point two. It is the manufacturer's own unreviewed study, and the group had nine women out of forty three.

00:45:40.621 --> 00:45:52.821
Its own subgroup was one point one on Monk skin tones one to four, and one point three on tones five and six. Most usefully, it says what it did not cover: tones seven to ten were not represented.

00:45:52.821 --> 00:46:06.771
So the question most in need of an answer, whether errors grow at the darkest skin tones, cannot be addressed by this study, and it acknowledges that other work disagrees on that point. I would like an independent test across the full scale.

00:46:07.421 --> 00:46:26.396
Zwift has launched a premium tier called Zwift Plus at thirty dollars or twenty seven pounds a month, against nineteen dollars for the existing tier, adding adaptive coaching and video routes. D C Rainmaker's account is that Zwift filmed its own routes rather than reusing those from Rouvy, the company it bought about six months ago.

00:46:26.396 --> 00:46:29.571
The aim is five to ten new routes a month.

00:46:35.761 --> 00:46:54.411
Gluck's Gym published two reviews since the last episode: Ranking the Best and Worst All In One Racks, on the second of November, and MX Select Evo, a budget adjustable dumbbell, on the twenty ninth of October. In both cases the automatic captions could not be downloaded when I tried, so I have no transcript.

00:46:54.411 --> 00:46:59.886
Both were published, and I could not read them, so there is nothing to report on what he found.

00:47:05.716 --> 00:47:12.291
A brief selection of what else the sources published, by title and description rather than from a full reading.

00:47:12.941 --> 00:47:33.316
The Real Science of Sport has an interview with Joel Mason on what working as a sports scientist is really like. Front Page Fitness discussed longevity fixation syndrome and grey market peptides marketed for menopause. Sigma Nutrition looked at three randomised trials of broad spectrum micronutrients for mood, in an episode for members.

00:47:33.316 --> 00:47:53.166
OMNIA Performance published pieces on V O two max and on hypertrophy for hybrid athletes. Running Explained wrote about missing a marathon goal time. Plant Based Health Professionals U K ran a two part podcast on what people get wrong about chicken, and a press item on doctors supporting Veganuary, which is advocacy.

00:47:53.166 --> 00:48:11.866
Michael Greger's site has a summary of a large observational study on swapping animal protein for plant protein, which I have not traced to the paper. The Physicians Committee's podcast has Kevin Hall on ultra processed food. D C Rainmaker covered Garmin bringing Edge ten sixty features to older units.

00:48:11.866 --> 00:48:18.466
And Marco Altini is co-editing a journal research topic on applied heart rate variability.

00:48:19.116 --> 00:48:35.716
That is the week. The main things to carry forward: two very different marathon preparations produced podium places on the same day, which tells nobody which is better; ferritin thresholds have moved on weak evidence; and a heart that thins with rest has not necessarily been cleared.
